Agent Memory Benchmark: A Manifesto | Hindsight - Vectorize
  URL: https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark
  [Skip to main content](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#__docusaurus_skipToContent_fallback)

New: **Introducing Hindsight Memlake**, serving 100M memories at <100ms p99 on object storage. [Read the post →](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/10/13/memlake)

![Agent Memory Benchmark: A Manifesto](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/assets/images/explorer-0-17c05c48af1e3f5dca49e469f2891e13.png)

We're building Hindsight to be the best AI memory system in the world. "Best" doesn't mean winning every benchmark. It means building something that genuinely works — and being honest about where it does and doesn't.

That's why we built Agent Memory Benchmark (AMB), and why we're making it fully open.

## "Best" is more than accuracy [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#best-is-more-than-accuracy)
- **Accuracy** — does the agent answer questions correctly using its memory?
- **Speed** — how long does retain and recall actually take?
- **Cost** — how many tokens does the system consume per operation?
- **Usability** — how much configuration, tuning, and infrastructure does it need to work?

## The problem with existing benchmarks [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#the-problem-with-existing-benchmarks)
LoComo and LongMemEval are solid datasets.

On most LoComo and LongMemEval instances, a naive "dump everything into context" approach scores competitively — not because it's a good memory architecture, but because retrieval has become the easy part.

This creates a false picture. The benchmark can no longer tell them apart.

That was the dominant paradigm when they were designed.

LoComo and LongMemEval are still a valid foundation — the question formats are good, the evaluation methodology is reasonable, and they remain useful for catching regressions.

## Datasets in AMB v1 [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#datasets-in-amb-v1)
| Dataset | What it stress-tests |
|-|-|
| **[LoComo](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17753)** | Multi-hop and temporal reasoning across long multi-session conversations |
| **[LongMemEval](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10813)** | Knowledge updates and belief revision — does the system handle facts that change over time? |

## Hindsight on the new baseline [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#hindsight-on-the-new-baseline)
| Dataset | Accuracy |
|-|-|
| LoComo | **92.0%** |
| LongMemEval | **94.6%** |
| LifeBench | **71.5%** |
| PersonaMem | **86.6%** |

- **Better retain process** — more accurate fact extraction means the right information gets stored in the first place; garbage in, garbage out applies directly to memory recall
- **Retrieval algorithm** — the retrieval pipeline has been substantially reworked, with meaningfully better accuracy, while preserving the same semantic interface that users already rely on

## Two modes, two tradeoffs [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#two-modes-two-tradeoffs)
LLM orchestration is evolving fast, and there isn't one right way to build a memory-augmented agent. AMB reflects that by supporting two distinct evaluation modes.

AMB lets you run both modes against the same dataset and compare the results directly — accuracy, latency, and token cost side by side — so you can make that tradeoff deliberately rather than by default.

## Try it [​](https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/03/23/agent-memory-benchmark#try-it)
AMB is live at **[agentmemorybenchmark.ai](https://agentmemorybenchmark.ai/)**.

The repo is at **[github.com/vectorize-io/agent-memory-benchmark](https://github.com/vectorize-io/agent-memory-benchmark)** — follow the instructions there to run the benchmarks against your own system and upload your results to the leaderboard.

We'd rather hear the hard feedback now than six months from now.

